#!/bin/bash
#
# ohmydebn-btop-run: Run btop inside the Super+T hotkey window
# (ohmydebn-btop-gui), restarting it in place when a theme change
# terminates it.
#
# ohmydebn-theme-set-btop sends btop SIGUSR2 to hot-reload its theme. That
# handler only exists on btop >= 1.3.1 - on anything older (Ubuntu 24.04 /
# Linux Mint 22 ship 1.3.0) SIGUSR2 has no handler and falls back to its
# POSIX default action, terminating btop. Running btop directly under
# `alacritty -e` (no --hold) means the window closes the instant that
# happens. The previous workaround killed and relaunched the window, which
# lost anything the user had done to it: a manual move or resize, the
# workspace it was on, the monitor it was on (gTile's window-created
# auto-tile re-places a fresh window on whichever monitor the pointer is
# on, in the rule's default grid slot).
#
# This loop keeps the same terminal window alive instead: when btop dies
# from SIGUSR2 specifically, run it again right here, in the same X window,
# so position/size/workspace/monitor/tiling are all untouched and the user
# sees one redraw in the new colors. btop re-initializes the terminal
# (raw mode, alternate screen, hidden cursor) on start, so the abrupt death
# of the previous instance leaves nothing to clean up. Every other exit -
# `q`, Escape, Ctrl+C, the window's close button, a crash - exits the loop
# with btop's own status so the window closes exactly as it always has.
# Nothing else on the system sends btop SIGUSR2, so this can never keep a
# window open that the user meant to close. On a btop that does hot-reload,
# the signal never terminates it and this loop simply never iterates.

# 128 + signal number is how bash reports a child killed by a signal.
# Looked up rather than hardcoded as 140 so it can't silently drift on an
# architecture with a different SIGUSR2 number.
SIGUSR2_STATUS=$((128 + $(kill -l USR2)))

while true; do
  /usr/bin/btop "$@"
  STATUS=$?
  [ "$STATUS" -eq "$SIGUSR2_STATUS" ] || exit "$STATUS"
done
